erms); Wollen v. Gulf Stream Restoration & Cleaning, LLC, 259 A.3d 867, 878\n(N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. 2021) (evaluating the totality of the circumstances and finding that the\npresentation of terms did not provide reasonable notice of seller's terms and conditions because\n\"[a]lthough the terms of the hyperlink, 'Terms & Conditions' were displayed in 'blue font' against\na white background, those terms were not underlined, bolded, or enlarged\"); Dickey v.\nTicketmaster LLC, 2019 WL 9096443 (C.D. Cal. Mar. 12, 2019) (holding that reasonably\nconspicuous, hyperlinked terms of use were enforceable because they were highly visible and\npresented in its typical blue color against a contrasting background); Peter v. Doordash, Inc., 445\nF. Supp. 3d 580 (N.D. Cal. 2020) (finding that notice was insufficient because, even though the\nscreen page with the terms and conditions was uncluttered and the notice of agreement to terms\nwas visible under the sign-up button, the hyperlinked terms were neither underlined nor\ncapitalized); Sarchi v. Uber Techs., Inc., 268 A.3d 258 (Me. 2022) (applying a \"totality of the\ncircumstances\" approach, analyzing the conspicuousness, design, placement, coloring, and\nsequence of notices during the registration process, and concluding that notice was insufficient).\nCourts also evaluate the reasonableness of a notice by considering whether the presentation\nof the standard terms comports with established market norms and the consumer expectations that\nsuch norms create. For example, the court in Cullinane v. Uber Technologies, Inc., 893 F.3d 53\n(1st Cir. 2018), held that notice was not reasonable because Uber did not employ the typical\nmethod of presenting hyperlinks (which are usually in contrasting color and underlined), thus\n56\n@§2\n"